---
import Marketing from '../layouts/Marketing.astro';
import PaymentProvider from '../components/PaymentProvider.astro';
import { providers } from '../data/providers';
import { paymentProvider } from '../data/paymentProvider';
---

<Marketing title="Privacy" description="MCP Warp privacy policy.">
  <section class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="effective-date"><strong>Effective date:</strong> October 8, 2026</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The short version.</strong> This summary is for convenience only — the full policy below is what applies.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>mcpwarp.io sets no cookies and uses no third-party analytics.</li>
        <li>The tunnel <strong>relays</strong> your MCP request and response bodies to your machine. To detect abuse, our systems automatically analyze every request and reply and keep per-account counters, not the content. Beyond a short-lived processing queue, bodies are stored, and can be viewed by a person, only if your account is flagged for abuse review — see "Traffic Monitoring" below.</li>
        <li>The dashboard at web.mcpwarp.io sets one httpOnly session cookie to keep you signed in.</li>
        <li>We collect account data (email, username, account identifier), your server's name, kind and status, monthly usage counters, subscription data from our payment provider, and personal access token hashes.</li>
        <li>Card details never touch our servers — our payment provider handles checkout and payment as merchant of record.</li>
        <li>Service logs and request traces are kept for 14 days. Performance metrics are aggregate, contain no personal data, and are deleted within 45 days. Traffic monitoring data has its own retention, listed in "Data Retention" below.</li>
        <li>Email <a href="mailto:support@mcpwarp.io">support@mcpwarp.io</a> to access, export, or delete the data we hold about you.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
      This Privacy Policy describes how mcpwarp.io ("Website"), the <code>mcpwarp</code> CLI
      ("Software"), and the MCP Warp dashboard and tunnel service (collectively, the "Service")
      handle your information.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Information We Collect</h2>

    <h3>Website</h3>
    <p>
      mcpwarp.io sets no cookies and uses no third-party analytics. Our hosting provider may
      collect technical information such as IP addresses in server logs as part of operating the
      infrastructure.
    </p>

    <h3>Dashboard Account</h3>
    <p>When you create an account at web.mcpwarp.io, we collect:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Authentication data:</strong> your email address, your username, and the account identifier issued by our authentication service, managed through our self-hosted Keycloak identity provider.</li>
      <li><strong>Cookies:</strong> web.mcpwarp.io sets one session cookie (<code>__Host-sid</code>, HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict, 30 days sliding) and a short-lived state cookie during sign-in. No analytics and no third-party scripts run on web.mcpwarp.io. No cookies are set on mcpwarp.io. Our payment provider's hosted checkout runs on its own domain under its own policy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>CLI</h3>
    <p>
      The <code>mcpwarp</code> CLI stores your login credentials locally (per-issuer, under
      <code>~/.mcpwarp/credentials/</code>). Logging in and running <code>mcpwarp up</code> sends
      your device-flow login and server registration requests to our backend, authenticated as
      your account.
    </p>

    <h3>Tunnel Connection Data</h3>
    <p>When you register a server and connect via <code>mcpwarp up</code>, we collect:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Server configuration:</strong> the server's name, its kind (stdio or HTTP), its status, and its public label <code>&lt;name&gt;-&lt;username&gt;</code>. We do not store the local command, path, port, or URL your server runs at — your CLI config never leaves your machine.</li>
      <li><strong>Connection state:</strong> whether your <code>mcpwarp up</code> process is currently connected, for routing requests and for display on your dashboard.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The tunnel forwards standard proxy headers (such as <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>) to your
      own MCP server, which runs on your machine; we never inject identity headers (email,
      username) into requests it receives.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloudflare, our network edge provider, may process connection metadata, including IP
      addresses, at the network edge under its own privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h3>Logging</h3>
    <p>
      Apart from the traffic monitoring described below, the tunnel does not keep an access log
      of MCP requests; it logs the client's IP address only on authentication failures. Our
      dashboard and API services keep standard HTTP access logs that include your IP address and
      browser user agent — authorization headers, cookies, and secrets are redacted from these
      logs. MCP request and response bodies are not logged. They pass briefly through the
      traffic monitoring queue and are kept longer only for accounts flagged for abuse review
      (see "Traffic Monitoring" below).
    </p>

    <h3>MCP Client OAuth</h3>
    <p>
      When an MCP client (such as Claude, ChatGPT, or VS Code) connects to your tunnel URL, it
      registers via OAuth 2.1 with dynamic client registration against our self-hosted Keycloak.
      We store the client registration and a mapping between that client and your tunnel so
      requests route correctly. We do not store OAuth access or refresh tokens issued to the
      client — those are managed by Keycloak.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you connect a client such as Claude or ChatGPT, you see a consent screen. The token
      issued to that client carries your account identifier, username, name, and email address,
      and is scoped to the single server you authorized.
    </p>

    <h3>Personal Access Tokens</h3>
    <p>
      Personal access tokens (<code>mcpwarp_pat_...</code>) are minted in the dashboard and shown
      to you once. We store a hash of the token, its name, a display prefix and last four
      characters, and creation, expiry, last-used, and revocation timestamps. The plaintext token
      is shown once and never stored.
    </p>

    <h3>Subscription Data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subscription tier:</strong> your current plan (Free, Plus, or Pro).</li>
      <li><strong>Tier history:</strong> an audit trail of plan changes with timestamps.</li>
      <li><strong>Expiration date:</strong> when your current subscription period ends (for paid plans).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Payments for paid plans are handled by our third-party payment provider; see the
      <a href="/terms/#payments-refunds-and-cancellation">Terms of Service (Payments section)</a>.
      Your payment details (card number, billing address, and related checkout information,
      which may include your IP address collected for tax-location and fraud-prevention
      purposes) are collected and processed by the payment provider on its own checkout pages
      under its own privacy policy. Card numbers never reach our servers and we do not store
      them.
    </p>
    <PaymentProvider context="privacy" />

    <h3>Service Monitoring</h3>
    <p>
      We use OpenTelemetry, self-hosted, to collect service health metrics and request traces
      (request paths, status codes, timing data). This does not capture the content of your MCP
      requests or responses. Performance metrics are aggregate counters and carry no per-user,
      per-server or per-IP labels. Per-account monitoring of tunnel traffic is a separate system,
      described next.
    </p>

    <h3 id="traffic-monitoring">Traffic Monitoring (Fair Use and Abuse Detection)</h3>
    <p>
      We run a self-hosted traffic monitoring service to detect abuse of the tunnel (for example,
      using it as a generic relay or proxy for traffic that isn't MCP), to detect use beyond fair
      use, and to protect the Service. It only measures and flags; it never blocks traffic or
      changes your account. It applies to every account.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Automated analysis, for all traffic.</strong> The tunnel sends a copy of every MCP
      request and reply that passes through your tunnel URL, body included (up to about 1 MiB),
      to the monitoring service through an internal message queue. The queue is written to disk
      on our infrastructure; each message is deleted once the service has processed it, and in
      any case within about 6 hours. The service analyzes each message automatically: it reads
      the time, the direction (request or reply), the JSON-RPC method and tool name, the HTTP
      status, the MCP protocol version, the body size, the client's user agent, the client's IP
      address (as reported by Cloudflare) and the identifier of the server the request went to,
      checks that the body is well-formed MCP, and measures how much of it is base64 or otherwise
      unpredictable data. For accounts that are not flagged, it stores only the results:
      per-account counters and size statistics per minute and per hour, and a record of which
      client IP addresses were seen on which day. It does not store the content or per-message
      details of your requests and replies, and no person looks at them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Flagging.</strong> Automatic rules flag an account when, within a time window, it
      exceeds a request count, a data volume, a share of requests left unanswered, or a number of
      distinct client IP addresses. Our administrators can also flag or unflag an account by
      hand, with a reason. A flag marks the account for human review; it does not suspend the
      account or block its traffic by itself. Any suspension is a separate decision under the
      <a href="/terms/#fair-use-and-abuse-monitoring">Terms of Service</a>. Both the monitoring
      service and our account database keep a record of each flag (the rule or reason, the
      measured value, the time, and who flagged or unflagged it), and the monitoring service keeps
      an audit record of each manual flag and unflag.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Stored content, only for flagged accounts.</strong> While an account is flagged,
      the service also stores a record of each message (the per-message details listed above,
      plus which protocol checks it failed) and the request and reply bodies themselves. Each body is stored up to its first
      64 KiB, and at most about 2,000 bodies or 200 MiB are kept per account; when either limit is
      reached, the oldest bodies are deleted first. Bodies contain whatever your MCP client and
      your server exchanged, which may include personal data. Only our administrators can view
      them, and only to investigate abuse. Capture stops when the account is unflagged; what was
      already stored stays until an administrator deletes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Legal basis.</strong> We process this data on the basis of our legitimate interest
      in preventing abuse and keeping the Service available and secure for everyone using it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Where it stays.</strong> The monitoring service, its queue and its database run on
      infrastructure controlled by MCP Warp, like the rest of the Service. Like our other
      databases, the monitoring database, including any stored bodies, is backed up to Cloudflare
      R2 storage (see "Third-Party Services" below). There is no self-service view of this data;
      to ask what we hold about your account, or to request its deletion, email
      <a href="mailto:support@mcpwarp.io">support@mcpwarp.io</a>.
      Limits, windows and retention periods are configuration and may change; the retention
      periods currently in effect are listed in "Data Retention" below.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Information We Do Not Collect</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The tunnel relays MCP request and response bodies between your MCP client and your local
        machine in real time. Our systems analyze this traffic automatically to compute abuse
        measurements, but we do <strong>not store its content or let a person view it</strong>,
        unless your account is flagged for abuse review. Bodies pass through an internal queue
        for at most about 6 hours — see "Traffic Monitoring" above.
      </li>
      <li>Card and payment details never touch our servers — see "Subscription Data" above.</li>
      <li>No third-party analytics trackers run on mcpwarp.io or the dashboard.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Third-Party Services</h2>
    <p>We rely on the following third-party and self-hosted services:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Service</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Privacy policy</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            providers.map((provider) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{provider.name}</td>
                <td>{provider.purpose}</td>
                <td>
                  {provider.selfHosted ? (
                    <>
                      Self-hosted on MCP Warp-controlled infrastructure
                      {provider.privacyUrl && (
                        <>
                          {' '}
                          (software: <a href={provider.privacyUrl}>{provider.name}</a>)
                        </>
                      )}
                    </>
                  ) : provider.privacyUrl ? (
                    <a href={provider.privacyUrl}>{provider.name} Privacy</a>
                  ) : (
                    'N/A'
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
          {paymentProvider && (
            <tr>
              <td>{paymentProvider.name}</td>
              <td>Merchant of record: payment processing and checkout (since {paymentProvider.since})</td>
              <td>
                <a href={paymentProvider.privacyUrl}>{paymentProvider.name} Privacy</a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          )}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      PostgreSQL, NATS, Keycloak, OpenTelemetry and the traffic monitoring service run on
      infrastructure controlled by MCP Warp. Database backups, including account and traffic
      monitoring data, are stored in Cloudflare R2 and kept for about 7 days.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Your Rights</h2>
    <p>You have the right to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Access your data</strong> — view your account information, server configuration, and subscription status through the dashboard.</li>
      <li><strong>Manage your data yourself</strong> — you can delete servers and revoke personal access tokens yourself in the dashboard at any time.</li>
      <li><strong>Access, export, or delete your account data</strong> — email <a href="mailto:support@mcpwarp.io">support@mcpwarp.io</a>. We handle these requests manually and confirm by email. This includes traffic monitoring data, which has no self-service view in the dashboard. We retain records we must keep for tax or accounting purposes.</li>
      <li><strong>Cancel your subscription</strong> — downgrade from a paid plan to Free at any time through the billing portal (Settings &gt; Billing &gt; Manage billing).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Data Retention</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Account data</strong> (email, username, account identifier) is retained for the lifetime of your account and deleted upon account deletion.</li>
      <li><strong>Server configuration</strong> is retained while your account is active and deleted when you remove a server or delete your account.</li>
      <li><strong>Service logs and request traces</strong> are retained for up to <strong>14 days</strong> and then automatically purged.</li>
      <li><strong>Performance metrics</strong> are aggregate counters that carry no user, server or IP identifiers. They are retained for 14 days and deleted in monthly batches, no later than <strong>45 days</strong> after collection.</li>
      <li><strong>Monthly usage counters and tier history</strong> are retained as billing records for the lifetime of your account, except records we are legally required to retain for longer (for example for tax or accounting purposes).</li>
      <li><strong>Personal access token hashes</strong> are retained until you revoke the token or delete your account.</li>
      <li><strong>Dashboard sessions</strong> are retained for up to <strong>30 days</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Traffic monitoring counters:</strong> per-minute counters are kept for about <strong>14 days</strong>, then rolled up into hourly counters, which are currently kept with no automatic expiry. Records of which client IP addresses were seen are kept for about <strong>45 days</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Tunnel request and response bodies</strong> pass through the monitoring queue, where each is deleted once processed and in any case within about <strong>6 hours</strong>. They are kept longer only if your account is flagged for abuse review: per-message records and stored bodies are then kept until an administrator deletes them, and bodies are also deleted oldest first once the per-account limit (about 2,000 bodies or 200 MiB) is reached.</li>
      <li><strong>Flag records:</strong> in the monitoring service, flag and audit records have no automatic expiry. The copy in our account database is kept until your account is deleted. Flag notifications on our internal message queue are kept for about <strong>7 days</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Traffic monitoring data and account deletion:</strong> the monitoring service is not tied to your account, so deleting your account does not delete its data automatically. On a deletion request, our administrators delete by hand all monitoring data held for your account: counters, IP records, flag and audit records, per-message records and stored bodies.</li>
      <li><strong>Database backups</strong>, including account and traffic monitoring data, are kept in Cloudflare R2 for about <strong>7 days</strong>, so deleted data can remain in backups for up to that long.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Children's Privacy</h2>
    <p>The Service is not directed at children under 13. We do not knowingly collect personal information from children.</p>

    <h2>7. Changes to This Policy</h2>
    <p>We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. Changes will be posted on this page with an updated effective date.</p>

    <h2>8. Contact</h2>
    <p>For privacy-related questions or requests, contact us at <a href="mailto:support@mcpwarp.io">support@mcpwarp.io</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Marketing>

<style>
  .legal {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 1.5rem 4rem;
    color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
  }
  .legal h1 {
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .effective-date {
    color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .legal h2 {
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
  }
  .legal h3 {
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .legal p,
  .legal li {
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .legal ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  .legal li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .legal a {
    color: var(--sl-color-accent-high);
  }
  .legal a:hover {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
  .legal code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
  .callout {
    border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-gray-5);
    background-color: var(--sl-color-gray-6);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .callout p {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
  }
  .callout ul {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--sl-color-gray-5);
    color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
  }
  th {
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
